WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.325
Week thirteen is different.

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:03.504
No guest speaker,

00:00:03.554 --> 00:00:06.783
just a Q and A with the unit coordinator about the final exam,

00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:09.322
plus Teresa's email to the class.

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:12.967
It's Monday the twenty-third of November at one pm.

00:00:13.017 --> 00:00:14.063
Venues vary,

00:00:14.113 --> 00:00:15.691
so check your own timetable,

00:00:15.741 --> 00:00:18.320
and find the personal email from the exams office.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:20.295
Look in junk too.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:21.800
Bring your own device.

00:00:21.283 --> 00:00:22.660
Your phone gets you the start passcode, then goes to the front desk.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:30.614
Your laptop needs Respondus lockdown software installed beforehand.

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.300
Before the day,

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.802
try the practice test on the exam site so the form isn't new to you.

00:00:35.852 --> 00:00:39.066
And only a pre-approved dictionary is allowed.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:42.795
The format is five short essay or scenario questions,

00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:43.856
six marks each,

00:00:43.906 --> 00:00:45.271
thirty marks in total.

00:00:45.321 --> 00:00:48.877
Answer every part, a, b and so on.

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:51.309
Aim for about three to four hundred words an answer,

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:53.656
and five hundred at most.

00:00:53.706 --> 00:00:57.093
There's no formal minimum, so keep it to the point.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.024
What's tested?

00:00:59.074 --> 00:01:03.564
The concepts, models and ideas from the guest lectures and the workshop recaps.

00:01:03.614 --> 00:01:07.150
Where a speaker named a model, expect a question on it.

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:09.279
What's not tested?

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:13.206
Speakers' career paths, exact dates, and full media plans.

00:01:13.256 --> 00:01:17.145
Background on a company will mostly be given to you in the question.

00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:19.929
Each question sticks to one lecture.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.193
Speakers wrote questions on their own talk,

00:01:22.243 --> 00:01:24.257
so learn each lecture's own concepts,

00:01:24.307 --> 00:01:26.271
even where themes overlap.

00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:29.205
Job roles aren't a framework to learn.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:30.055
But a question could describe a company problem and ask who would be involved,

00:01:29.396 --> 00:01:34.973
and what process they'd follow.

00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:37.492
The big skill is transfer.

00:01:37.542 --> 00:01:41.148
A concept a speaker showed with Telstra might be asked about Qantas.

00:01:41.198 --> 00:01:44.297
Know the concept, not just the example.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.330
Watch the wording.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:49.184
Why did X do this wants the speaker's reasoning.

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:52.670
Could Y do it too wants your own justified view.

00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:57.303
There's no universal marking formula and no fixed one mark per element.

00:01:57.353 --> 00:01:58.600
Follow the exact task and every subpart, and be precise.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:03.941
A formal essay structure isn't needed.

00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:05.927
So for each answer:

00:02:05.977 --> 00:02:07.519
name and explain the concept,

00:02:07.569 --> 00:02:08.722
apply it step by step to the brand in the question,

00:02:08.772 --> 00:02:11.458
then link it to the outcome.

00:02:11.508 --> 00:02:13.863
An opinion alone won't cut it.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:15.472
To revise,

00:02:15.522 --> 00:02:17.265
go back through every lecture's notes,

00:02:17.315 --> 00:02:18.115
the workshop and tutorial questions,

00:02:17.999 --> 00:02:20.496
and the pre-work.

00:02:20.546 --> 00:02:24.421
Focus on the lectures, not your report or presentation.

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:26.333
And don't bother asking your tutor what's in the exam.

00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:31.003
Tutors genuinely don't know the questions.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.500
On the day,

00:02:32.514 --> 00:02:34.374
have Respondus installed and tested,

00:02:34.424 --> 00:02:36.332
your laptop charged with the charger packed,

00:02:36.382 --> 00:02:37.688
your phone for the passcode,

00:02:37.738 --> 00:02:38.973
and your venue checked.

00:02:39.023 --> 00:02:40.642
Arrive early.

00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:45.658
So: five questions, six marks each, one lecture per question.

00:02:45.708 --> 00:02:49.667
Know each concept well enough to use it on a brand the speaker never mentioned.

00:02:49.717 --> 00:02:50.634
Good luck.

00:02:50.684 --> 00:02:52.651
That's week thirteen.
